+Where the plan's last stage is the release's own install (the release stamped, installed into the host, and tried on a project that uses it), the stage review of the release's text runs before the stamp, so that what is stamped is what both reviewers read; a correction to text alone found after the stamp rides with the next release, unless it changes what a project does, which takes a fix release of its own; and the release's field acceptance names what may stand in for it while the trial project has no increment at the gate (a probe of the installed release on the conditions it fixes, say), the acceptance itself recorded when that increment comes (P17).
+
 ## Add at delegation-loop.test-checks
 
 Beside the tests that run in isolation, each behaviour of the part that depends on the host is tested inside the real host, at the version the design's host section records, with the part installed into a host kept apart from the owner's own (a separate profile, folder or instance, as the host allows), never into the host the owner works in. The behaviour is driven there and its result read from what the host did. A host behaviour no test can reach inside the host is named in the increment's report as untested there, never counted as tested. Where driving the host uses the owner's account, that use is put to the owner once, before any test relies on it, and what it reaches is recorded in `docs/INVENTORY.md` (Decisions), a use beyond it asked again; where it draws paid usage, the part is classified with *spends money* too, and each run that draws it is a paid action of that trait's, approved for itself and run by the supervisor, never by the builder or in CI.
